  <content>
  <section><title>Solving Quadratic Equations of the Form \(a(x - h)^2 = k\)</title>
<para>We can use the square root property to solve an equation of the form \(a(x-h)^2=k\) as well.</para>
<para>Notice that the quadratic term, \(x\), in the original form \(ax^2=k\) is replaced with \((x−h)\).</para>
<para>\(a {\style{color:red}x}^{2}=k \quad a{\style{color:red}(}{\style{color:red}x}\;{\style{color:red}-}\;{\style{color:red}h}{\style{color:red})}^{2}=k\)</para>
<para>Like before, the first step is to isolate the term that has the variable squared. In this case, a binomial is being squared.</para>
<para>Once the binomial is isolated, by dividing each side by the coefficient of \(a\), the square root property can be used on \((x−h)^2\).</para>
<para>Solve \(4(y−7)^2=48\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Isolate the quadratic term and make its coefficient 1.</para>
<para> So, divide both sides of the equation by 4.</para>
<para>\(4(y−7)^2=48\)
  \((y−7)^2=12\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Use the square root property on the binomial.</para>
<para>  \(y−7= \pm \sqrt{12}\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Simplify the radical by pulling out the factors that are perfect squares.</para>
<para>  Solve for \(y\). Rewrite the two solutions.</para>
<para>\(y−7= \pm \sqrt{12}\)
  \(y−7= \pm \sqrt{(4 \cdot 3})\)
  \(y−7= \pm 2 \sqrt{3}\)
  \(y =7 \pm 2 \sqrt{3}\)
  \(y =7+2 \sqrt{3}\), \(y=7−2 \sqrt{3}\) </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4 </emphasis>
  Check the solutions.</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rrr} 4(y-7)^{2}=48 &amp; 4(y-7)^{2}=48 \\ 4({\style{color:red}7}\;{\style{color:red}+}\;{\style{color:red}2} \sqrt{{\style{color:red}3}}-7)^{2} \stackrel{?}{=} 48 &amp; 4({\style{color:red}7}\;{\style{color:red}-}\;{\style{color:red}2} \sqrt{{\style{color:red}3}}-7)^{2} \stackrel{?}{=} 48 \\ 4(2 \sqrt{3})^{2} \stackrel{?}{=} 48 &amp; 4(-2 \sqrt{3})^{2} \stackrel{?}{=} 48 \\ 4(12)^{2} \stackrel{?}{=} 48 &amp; 4(12)^{2} \stackrel{?}{=} 48 \\ 48=48 {\style{color:red}\checkmark} &amp; 48=48 {\style{color:red}\checkmark} \end{array} \)</para>
<title>Try It: Solving Quadratic Equations of the Form \(a(x-h)^2=k\)</title>
<para>Solve the following quadratic equations.</para>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>\(3(a−3)^2=363\) </para>
    <para> \(\frac{1}{2} (b+2)^2=200\) </para>
    <para>Write down your answers, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Here is how to solve these quadratic equations of the form \(a(x-h)^2=k\):</para>
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item> \(3(a−3)^2 =363\) 
      \((a−3)^2=121\)
      \(a−3= \pm 11\)
      \(a−3=11\)      \(a−3=-11\)
      \(a= 14\) and \(a=-8\)</item>
  </list>
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item> \(\frac{1}{2}(b+2)^2=200\)
      \((b+2)^2=400\)
      \(b+2= \pm 20\)
      \(b+2=20\)      \(b+2=-20\)
      \(b=18\) and \(b=-22\)</item>
  </list>
</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
